no(321,'Both zinc carbonate and lead carbonate can leave yellow oxides on heating, and their hydroxides dissolve in excess alkali. Insoluble carbonates also require a dissolved-ion preparation step before the proposed precipitation test. The source does not supply that condition or distinguish hot/cold residue colour; old aluminium answer contradicts the yellow residue. Hold for a complete unambiguous source.')
yes(322,'C','The longest chain containing the double bond has six carbons. The double bond is at position 3 from either end. Numbering from the nearer branched end gives methyl groups at positions 2 and 4, rather than 3 and 5, so the name is 2,4-dimethylhex-3-ene.',[ORG],question='What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂C(CH₃)=CHCH(CH₃)CH₃?',options=['2-Ethyl-4-methylpent-2-ene','3,5-Dimethylhex-3-ene','2,4-Dimethylhex-3-ene','2-Methyl-4-ethylpent-3-ene'],audit='Visually recovered complete branched structure from PDFpage29 into equivalent condensed notation; corrected dimethyl spelling without changing locants.',check='branched-alkene')
no(323,'Sourcepage29 prints an invalid CH₃≡CH substrate and omits the reagent over the arrow. Product structures are also incompletely extracted. Sodium propynide cannot be inferred from the old key or invented terminal-alkyne chemistry. Need legible original substrate, reagent and full structures before publication.')
yes(324,'C','An alkanol has an O–H group that reacts with sodium to form an alkoxide and hydrogen gas. Alkanols are ordinarily neutral to litmus. Among the four listed classes, this combination of observations identifies an alkanol.',[RX],question='A clear organic liquid is neutral to litmus and gives a colourless gas when it reacts with metallic sodium. Which listed class does it belong to?',options=['Alkanoate','Alkene','Alkanol','Alkane'],audit='Removed embedded page marker; scoped identification to the four listed classes, not all possible neutral organic liquids.')
yes(325,'B','Sodium hydroxide removes one acidic proton from oxalic acid. The products are sodium hydrogen oxalate and water: HOOC–COOH + NaOH → HOOC–COONa + H₂O. This acid–base reaction is neutralisation.',[RX],question='HOOC–COOH + NaOH → HOOC–COONa + H₂O. What type of reaction is shown?',options=['Displacement','Neutralisation','Elimination','Saponification'],audit='Visually restored both oxalic-acid structures and arrow from PDFpage30 as complete condensed formulae; one equivalent neutralises one proton.',check='oxalic-neutralization')
no(326,'Source repeats alkanoic instead of the comparison class and merges optionB intoA. Its claimed contrast of exactly two acid hydrogen bonds versus exactly one alcohol hydrogen bond is not a general molecular rule. Both classes hydrogen-bond and polarity also affects volatility. Need a sound original comparison; do not teach false bond-count absolutes.')
no(327,'Concentrated sulfuric acid dehydrates sugar to carbonaceous residue but the exothermic chemistry can also generate toxic fumes and carbon dioxide. The broad which-is-formed stem offers both carbon residue and toxic fumes. Restricting it to the solid residue would add a missing selection condition. Retain privately until original intended observation is established.',answer='C for solid residue only')
yes(328,'A','Liquid air contains substances with different boiling points. Fractional distillation separates its components, allowing nitrogen to be collected separately from oxygen and other gases. The other listed tasks chiefly involve separating dissolved solids from their solvents.',[BOOK],question='Which separation is carried out by fractional distillation?',options=['Nitrogen from liquid air','Sodium chloride from seawater','Iodine from a solution in carbon tetrachloride','Sulfur from a solution in carbon disulfide'],audit='Corrected fraction of distillation to fractional distillation and for sea water to from seawater.')
no(329,'The source gives carbon(II) sulphides, not a valid unambiguous name for the intended compound. A matching Myschool47343 transcription says carbon sulphide and Vulcanizer’s solution but labels another year; it does not independently establish carbon disulfide or oxidation numberIV. Do not replace the compound solely because the intended mixture keyA is plausible. Recover exact compound identity first.',answer='A conditional on a definite pure carbon sulfide compound')
yes(330,'B','The ore contains 80×0.700 = 56.0 kg of Fe₂O₃. Iron contributes 2×56 = 112 of its relative formula mass 160. The theoretical iron mass is therefore 56.0×112/160 = 39.2 kg.',[RX,'https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/26/'],question='An iron ore contains 70.0% Fe₂O₃ by mass. What mass of iron can theoretically be obtained from 80 kg of this ore? [Fe = 56, O = 16]',options=['35.0 kg','39.2 kg','70.0 kg','78.4 kg'],audit='Recovered conventional rounded Fe relative atomic mass56 from established iron atomic data; source356 is not a possible conventional atomic weight. No experimental amount changed. Removed constants from optionD.',check='iron-ore')
yes(331,'C','The chlorine-to-metal mass ratios are 0.36/0.20 = 1.80 and 0.71/0.40 = 1.775. These are approximately the same at the precision of the reported masses. The data therefore support constant composition: the elements combine in essentially the same mass proportion.',[RX,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/12984?exam_year=1991'],question='In two experiments, 0.36 g and 0.71 g of chlorine combine with 0.20 g and 0.40 g of a metal X, respectively. Which law is supported by these mass data, allowing for their reported precision?',options=['Multiple proportions','Conservation of mass','Constant composition','Reciprocal proportions'],audit='Preserved all four masses. Corrected oldA toC; ratios are near1:1 after normalisation, not evidence of distinct stoichiometries. Explicitly stated reported-precision interpretation and corrected conversation to conservation.',check='constant-composition')
no(332,'Boyle law with30cm³ at10atm and final20dm³ gives0.015atm, absent from all four options. Changing dm³ tocm³ would give15atm but no original source establishes that change. Old explanation selects6atm despite its own correct arithmetic; no nearest or invented key.',answer='0.015atm; none listed')
yes(333,'C','The balanced decomposition is 2KHCO₃ → K₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂. KHCO₃ has molar mass 100 g mol⁻¹, so 1 kg is 10 mol. Every two moles give one mole of CO₂; 5 mol therefore occupy 5×22.4 = 112 dm³ at STP.',[RX],question='What volume of CO₂ at STP is produced by complete thermal decomposition of 1 kg of KHCO₃? [K = 39, H = 1, C = 12, O = 16; molar gas volume at STP = 22.4 dm³ mol⁻¹]',options=['28 dm³','56 dm³','112 dm³','196 dm³'],audit='Moved constants from optionD to stem; replaced contradictory one-to-one intermediate calculation with balanced stoichiometry.',check='bicarbonate-gas')
yes(334,'A','In the expected electron-transfer model, X loses two electrons to form X²⁺ and each Y gains one to form Y⁻. Charge balance requires two Y⁻ ions for each X²⁺, giving XY₂. A formula of XY would not be electrically neutral.',[ATOM],question='Atoms of X have two outer-shell electrons and atoms of Y have seven. For their expected ionic compound, which statement is false?',options=['It has formula XY','It is likely to be ionic','It contains X²⁺ ions','It contains Y⁻ ions'],audit='Made the source ionic electron-transfer model explicit; not an assertion that every element with a filled two-electron shell formsX²⁺.',check='ionic-charge')
yes(335,'D','An X⁻ ion has gained one electron, so its 10 electrons correspond to 9 protons. A Y⁺ ion has lost one electron, so its 10 electrons correspond to 11 protons. Ion formation changes electron count, not the number of protons in the nucleus.',[ATOM],question='X⁻ and Y⁺ each contain 10 electrons. How many protons are in the nuclei of X and Y, respectively?',options=['10 and 10','9 and 9','11 and 9','9 and 11'],audit='Corrected proteins to protons and old keyC toD; existing explanation already contradicted oldkey.',check='isoelectronic-ions')
yes(336,'C','Ammonium chloride is an ionic solid of NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻ ions. Within NH₄⁺, the N–H bonds are covalent; formation from NH₃ and H⁺ can be described as donation of a nitrogen lone pair, or dative covalent bonding. Once NH₄⁺ has formed, its four N–H bonds are equivalent.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-summary'],question='Which listed combination describes bonding in ammonium chloride, including the electron-pair donation used to form NH₄⁺?',options=['Ionic only','Covalent only','Ionic and dative covalent','Dative covalent only'],audit='Replaced molecule with ionic-solid framing and clarified dative as bond-formation electron provenance, not a physically distinct N–H bond in the resulting ion.')
yes(337,'B','All five elements lie in period 3. Electronegativity generally increases from left to right across this period, so the order is Na < Mg < Al < P < Cl. Chlorine attracts shared bonding electrons most strongly in this list.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-5-periodic-variations-in-element-properties'],question='Which sequence is in increasing order of electronegativity?',options=['Chlorine, aluminium, magnesium, phosphorus, sodium','Sodium, magnesium, aluminium, phosphorus, chlorine','Chlorine, phosphorus, aluminium, magnesium, sodium','Sodium, chlorine, phosphorus, magnesium, aluminium'])
no(338,'The item requires two electron-shell diagrams absent from normalized content. Sourcepage31 shell dots are insufficiently legible to count with confidence. Old explanation guesses covalent from a typical-question pattern. Hold until both atom shell populations can be faithfully recovered; no diagram-dependent publication.')
yes(339,'B','Mercury ions are toxic contaminants and are a concern in drinking water at low concentrations. Calcium and magnesium commonly contribute to water hardness, while iron can affect colour and taste. Mercury is the toxic-metal contaminant identified by this comparison.',['https://www.epa.gov/mercury/basic-information-about-mercury'],question='Which listed ion is a toxic mercury contaminant of drinking water?',options=['Ca²⁺','Hg²⁺','Mg²⁺','Fe²⁺'],audit='Removed unsupported claim that any trace quantity necessarily causes harm; retained identity question but mercury named in stem would reveal answer, therefore final stem revised below.')
