yes(344,'B','Sulfur in H₂S changes from −2 to 0, so H₂S is oxidised and acts as the reductant. Sulfur in SO₂ changes from +4 to 0, so SO₂ is reduced and acts as the oxidant. Two sulfide sulfur atoms each lose two electrons, balancing the four electrons gained by the SO₂ sulfur atom.',[RX,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13064?c_page=6&exam_type=jamb&page=146'],question='2H₂S(g) + SO₂(g) → 3S(s) + 2H₂O(l). Which statement describes this reaction?',options=['It is redox: H₂S is the oxidant and SO₂ is the reductant','It is redox: SO₂ is the oxidant and H₂S is the reductant','It is not redox because there is no oxidant','It is not redox because there is no reductant'],audit='Exact matching Myschool13064 transcription independently suppliesH₂S missing from PDFH₂. Restored from matching source, not inferred solely from atom balance or key.',check='sulfur-redox')
yes(345,'C','MnO₂ acts as a catalyst. It provides a reaction pathway with a lower activation energy, so more reacting particles can overcome the energy barrier. A catalyst does not change the overall enthalpy change of the reaction.',['https://edu.rsc.org/exhibition-chemistry/the-genie-in-the-bottle/3009156.article','https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis'],question='MnO₂ hastens hydrogen peroxide decomposition mainly by',options=['Increasing reactant surface area','Increasing reactant concentration','Lowering the activation energy','Lowering the reaction enthalpy'])
no(346,'Even provisionally interpreting broken heat-capacity units as4.18Jg⁻¹K⁻¹,50g water and34°C rise absorb7106J. Dividing by1.1/111mol gives about−717kJmol⁻¹, not−71.1. Source does not justify changing34 to3.4 or1.1g to11g. Hold missing units and inconsistent data/options; no factor-ten rescue.',answer='About−717kJmol⁻¹ under stated conventional assumptions; none listed')
yes(347,'A','The negative enthalpy change means the forward reaction releases heat, so lowering temperature favours products. There are 2 moles of reactant gases and 1.5 moles of product gases per equation. Higher pressure therefore also favours products, which occupy the smaller gas volume.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-3-shifting-equilibria-le-chateliers-principle'],question='NO(g) + CO(g) ⇌ ½N₂(g) + CO₂(g), ΔH = −89.3 kJ. Which conditions favour the greatest equilibrium conversion of NO and CO to products?',options=['Low temperature and high pressure','High temperature and low pressure','High temperature and high pressure','Low temperature and low pressure'],audit='Restored arrow and standard gaseous states for these gaseous species; corrected old claim of equal gas mole totals. Equilibrium conversion, not reaction speed, is asked.',check='gas-equilibrium')
no(348,'All physical states are missing. H₂+I₂⇌2HI has equal gas moles only when iodine is gaseous; condensed iodine changes that conclusion. OptionA also depends on condensed versus gas states. Need actual states rather than assume all gases to support oldB.',answer='B only for all-gaseous H₂/I₂/HI equilibrium')
yes(349,'C','The rate ratio is (1.2×10⁻⁴)/(3.0×10⁻⁵) = 4. Thus, when the listed initial NO concentration doubles, the measured initial rate increases fourfold.',[RX],question='At 25 °C, doubling the initial NO concentration from the first measurement to the second changes the initial rate of its reaction with chlorine from 3.0×10⁻⁵ to 1.2×10⁻⁴ mol s⁻¹. By what factor does the rate increase?',options=['Two','Three','Four','Five'],audit='Converted legible two-row table to text without inventing missing concentration-volume units. Preserved doubling and both measured rates; did not claim kinetic order without controlled other-reactant concentrations.',check='rate-ratio')
yes(350,'C','Nitrous oxide, N₂O, can support combustion and relight a glowing splint. At the hot splint it can decompose to nitrogen and oxygen. The glowing-splint observation is therefore not exclusive to a sample of pure oxygen.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/18-7-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-nitrogen'],question='Which listed gas can rekindle a brightly glowing splint?',options=['NO₂','NO','N₂O','Cl₂'])
yes(351,'A','Sodium carbonate is thermally stable enough to melt during ordinary heating. Calcium, magnesium and zinc carbonates instead undergo thermal decomposition to their oxides and carbon dioxide before they can simply be melted under ordinary laboratory conditions.',[BOOK],question='Which listed salt can be melted without decomposition under ordinary laboratory conditions?',options=['Na₂CO₃','CaCO₃','MgCO₃','ZnCO₃'],audit='Made ordinary laboratory conditions explicit; no claim about all extreme pressures or temperatures.')
no(352,'The general heating premise is not selective:KNO₃ yields oxygen on decomposition, but sufficiently heatedMnO₂ can also lose oxygen. Ammonium nitrate pathways vary with conditions, and optionB has an invalid ammonium trioxonitrate(III) name. Need complete original conditions/options; do not selectC while teachingD can never evolve oxygen.')
no(353,'The essential FeS/HCl gas-generation apparatus is absent from normalized stem, and test-paper optionB has corrupted dichromate oxidation state. The old explanation falsely identifies oxygen. Lead-salt paper detectingH₂S is plausible but complete diagram and distinct test observations must be recovered before publication.',answer='C conditional on full H₂S apparatus/test wording')
yes(354,'C','Aqueous ammonia first supplies hydroxide ions, forming white Zn(OH)₂. In excess ammonia, zinc forms the soluble complex ion [Zn(NH₃)₄]²⁺, so the precipitate dissolves. This ligand-complex formation explains the observation.',['https://edu.rsc.org/experiments/transition-elements-and-complex-compounds-microscale-experiment-16-18-years/518.article'],question='Why does the white precipitate produced by adding aqueous ammonia to Zn²⁺ solution dissolve in excess ammonia?',options=['Zinc is amphoteric','Zinc hydroxide is readily soluble','Zinc forms a complex soluble in excess ammonia','Ammonia solution is a strong base'],audit='Independently distinguished ammonia complex formation from amphoteric dissolution in excess strong hydroxide.')
yes(355,'C','A small amount of CO₂ reacts with limewater, Ca(OH)₂(aq), to form insoluble calcium carbonate: Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O. The CaCO₃ is the white precipitate. With prolonged excess CO₂, that precipitate can dissolve again.',[RX],question='Which substance in clear aqueous solution forms a white precipitate when CO₂ is bubbled through it for a short time?',options=['KOH','NaOH','Ca(OH)₂','Al(OH)₃'],check='limewater')
no(356,'Copper sulfate is used as a fungicide, but it also has agricultural micronutrient-fertilizer and disinfection uses. The broad widely-used-as stem does not specify a context that excludes these other listed applications. Hold rather than add a fungal-crop context solely to forceB.',answer='B is a common use, not uniquely specified by the stem')
yes(357,'C','Silver and mercury nitrates can ultimately yield their metals on strong heating because the corresponding oxides are thermally unstable. Copper and magnesium nitrates instead leave stable metal oxides under the usual comparison conditions. The pair is therefore mercury and silver.',[BOOK],question='Which pair of metals can be obtained by thermal decomposition of their nitrate salts?',options=['Copper and mercury','Silver and copper','Mercury and silver','Magnesium and mercury'],audit='Corrected nitrate spelling and malformed preposition; explained ultimate metal formation through unstable oxides rather than old unbalanced mercury equation.',check='nitrate-balance')
yes(358,'B','Each carbon of the double bond in but-2-ene has one H and one CH₃ group. Restricted rotation allows distinct cis and trans arrangements. But-1-ene and 2-methylbut-2-ene have identical groups on one double-bond carbon, while CH₂BrCl has no double bond.',[ORG],question='Which compound can exist as geometric isomers?',options=['2-Methylbut-2-ene','But-2-ene','But-1-ene','CH₂BrCl'],audit='Visually restored optionD from PDFpage33 as complete equivalent condensed formula; no absent figure dependency.')
yes(360,'D','Propyne has molar mass 3×12 + 4×1 = 40 g mol⁻¹, so 10 g is 0.25 mol. Complete addition across its triple bond uses 2 mol of Br₂ per mole of propyne. The bromine mass is 0.25×2×160 = 80 g.',['https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/9-3-reactions-of-alkynes-addition-of-hx-and-x2'],question='What mass of bromine is required for complete addition to 10 g of propyne? [C = 12, H = 1, Br = 80]',options=['20 g','40 g','60 g','80 g'],audit='Corrected oldB toD and false four-Br₂ coefficient; preserved all source masses and constants.',check='propyne-bromination')
